# tools/b/branch_witness.rish -- the skeleton junction detector proves itself on metal (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the branch / junction detector the endpoint rung named, the other half of skeleton
# topology. A skeleton reads a shape down to a one-pixel medial figure; where image/endpoints.rye answers WHERE it ends
# (degree one), this rung answers WHERE it branches. A JUNCTION is a foreground pixel where three or more branches meet,
# read by the CROSSING NUMBER (the count of foreground arcs around the eight-neighbor ring) rather than a template pack
# (degree three or more spans 219 configs) or a naive neighbor count (which false-flags the flankers of an orthogonal junction).
#
# The crux this witness proves on metal: a diagonal Y lights exactly its one meeting point of crossing number three and leaves
# its endpoints and path pixels dark; a one-pixel plus lights only its degree-four center while the four pixels flanking it
# (naive degree four, crossing number two) stay dark; a lone dot and a solid block interior both light nothing (crossing
# number zero); the output is opaque white-on-black even from a translucent source; the junction map round-trips through the
# open QOI codec byte-for-byte; an empty source refuses by name; the same detection is deterministic. No network, no key, no
# funds -- the branch points a surface names the day it reads a skeleton's topology, on our own open geometry.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/b/branch_witness.rish

say "branch: Language EN -- Season G junction detector; mark every skeleton junction of a mask (a foreground pixel where three or more branches meet) by the crossing number."
say "branch: Style Radiant -- compose not braid: this rung owns only the crossing-number gather (the eight-neighbor ring and its arc count) and the degree threshold; hitmiss decides the binary meaning of a pixel, qoi owns the buffers."
say "branch: Lens TAME -- the binary meaning of a pixel is read from hitmiss (one source of truth); an out-of-bounds neighbor is ground never a phantom; the source is refused by name up front so a refusal allocates nothing; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/branch"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/branch.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "branch: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "branch: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "branch: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-branch" else "branch: selftest did not print the GREEN line"

say "branch: GREEN -- a diagonal Y lights its one meeting point, a plus only its center (the flankers a naive degree count would flag stay dark), a lone dot and a solid interior nothing, the crossing number reading the genuine skeleton topology, on metal."
